        "effective_step": 1,
        "end_token_index": 41,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 42,
        "step_number": 1,
        "text": "Step 1: Let \\( y = \\arccos x \\). Then the given equation becomes \\( y + \\arccos 2x + \\arccos 3x = \\pi \\)."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 264,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 266,
        "char_start": 107,
        "effective_step": 2,
        "end_token_index": 106,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 107,
        "step_number": 2,
        "text": "Step 2: Since \\( y = \\arccos x \\), we have \\( x = \\cos y \\). The equation transforms to \\( \\arccos (\\cos y) + \\arccos (2 \\cos y) + \\arccos (3 \\cos y) = \\pi \\)."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 423,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 425,
        "char_start": 268,
        "effective_step": 3,
        "end_token_index": 172,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 173,
        "step_number": 3,
        "text": "Step 3: Knowing that \\( \\arccos (\\cos y) = y \\) for \\( y \\in [0, \\pi] \\), the equation simplifies to \\( y + \\arccos (2 \\cos y) + \\arccos (3 \\cos y) = \\pi \\)."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 506,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 508,
        "char_start": 427,
        "effective_step": 4,
        "end_token_index": 209,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 210,
        "step_number": 4,
        "text": "Step 4: Rearrange to get \\( \\arccos (2 \\cos y) + \\arccos (3 \\cos y) = \\pi - y \\)."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 681,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 683,
        "char_start": 510,
        "effective_step": 5,
        "end_token_index": 285,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 286,
        "step_number": 5,
        "text": "Step 5: Use the identity \\( \\arccos A + \\arccos B = \\pi \\) if and only if \\( AB = -1 \\). Here, \\( A = 2 \\cos y \\) and \\( B = 3 \\cos y \\), so \\( (2 \\cos y)(3 \\cos y) = -1 \\)."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 867,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 869,
        "char_start": 685,
        "effective_step": 6,
        "end_token_index": 340,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 341,
        "step_number": 6,
        "text": "Step 6: This simplifies to \\( 6 \\cos^2 y = -1 \\), which is impossible since \\( \\cos^2 y \\geq 0 \\). Therefore, we need to consider the range restrictions and possible values of \\( y \\)."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1030,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1032,
        "char_start": 871,
        "effective_step": 7,
        "end_token_index": 411,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 412,
        "step_number": 7,
        "text": "Step 7: Instead, use the fact that \\( \\arccos (2 \\cos y) + \\arccos (3 \\cos y) = \\pi - y \\) implies \\( \\cos(\\arccos (2 \\cos y) + \\arccos (3 \\cos y)) = -\\cos y \\)."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1200,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1202,
        "char_start": 1034,
        "effective_step": 8,
        "end_token_index": 486,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 487,
        "step_number": 8,
        "text": "Step 8: Using the cosine addition formula, \\( \\cos(\\arccos (2 \\cos y) + \\arccos (3 \\cos y)) = (2 \\cos y)(3 \\cos y) - \\sqrt{1 - (2 \\cos y)^2} \\sqrt{1 - (3 \\cos y)^2} \\)."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1359,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1361,
        "char_start": 1204,
        "effective_step": 9,
        "end_token_index": 547,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 548,
        "step_number": 9,
        "text": "Step 9: Set this equal to \\( -\\cos y \\) and solve for \\( \\cos y \\). After simplifying, we find that \\( \\cos y = -\\frac{1}{2} \\), so \\( y = \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\)."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1465,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1467,
        "char_start": 1363,
        "effective_step": 10,
        "end_token_index": 596,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 597,
        "step_number": 10,
        "text": "Step 10: Since \\( y = \\arccos x \\), we have \\( x = \\cos \\left( \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\right) = -\\frac{1}{2} \\)."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1654,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1655,
        "char_start": 1469,
        "effective_step": 11,
        "end_token_index": 664,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 665,
        "step_number": 11,
        "text": "Step 11: Substitute \\( x = -\\frac{1}{2} \\) into the original polynomial form. We need to find a cubic polynomial \\( ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0 \\) that has \\( x = -\\frac{1}{2} \\) as a root."
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 2090,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 2091,
        "char_start": 1657,
        "effective_step": 12,
        "end_token_index": 877,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 878,
        "step_number": 12,
        "text": "Step 12: Assume the polynomial is \\( 2x^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0 \\) (choosing \\( a = 2 \\) to simplify calculations). Substituting \\( x = -\\frac{1}{2} \\):\n\n\\[ 2 \\left( -\\frac{1}{2} \\right)^3 + b \\left( -\\frac{1}{2} \\right)^2 + c \\left( -\\frac{1}{2} \\right) + d = 0 \\]\n\\[ 2 \\left( -\\frac{1}{8} \\right) + b \\left( \\frac{1}{4} \\right) - \\frac{c}{2} + d = 0 \\]\n\\[ -\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{b}{4} - \\frac{c}{2} + d = 0 \\]\n\\[ -1 + b - 2c + 4d = 0 \\]"
      },
      {
